#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230152 :tutorial/content "When solving ODEs numerically:\n\n- The solution is advanced in many steps.\n\n- Each step introduces a small error (LTE).\n\n- These errors propagate forward (contributing to GTE).\n\nStability describes how a numerical method responds to these small perturbations.\n\nA method is **stable** if small errors remain controlled as the computation proceeds.\nA method is **unstable** if small errors can grow rapidly, even when the true solution stays well-behaved.\n\nStability determines how local errors turn into global errors: a method may be accurate in a single step, but if it is unstable, the global error can grow without bound.\n\nOne way to study stability is to test a numerical method on the following **test equation**:\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0)=1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0\n$$\n\nThe exact solution of the test equation is $y(x) = e^{-\\lambda x},$ which satisfies $y(x) \\to 0$ as $x \\to \\infty.$\n\nWe say that a numerical method with update formula \n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = y_n + \\Delta y\n$$\n\n is **stable** for a given step size $\\Delta x$ if, when applied to the test equation, the numerical solution also satisfies\n\n$$\ny_n \\to 0 \\:\\text{ as }\\: n \\to \\infty.\n$$\n\nTo see how this works in practice, let's analyze the stability of Euler's method." :tutorial/id #uuid "ac3e5ad1-de8e-5400-81a3-ea12a0eae4b8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14217 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230181 :tutorial/content "We will now check the stability of Euler's method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0)=1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that, for an initial value problem $y' = f(x,y), y(x_0) = y_0,$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = y_n + f(x_n, y_n) \\cdot \\Delta x.\n$$\n\nApplying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-\\lambda {y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-\\lambda {y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for stability, we require $y_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty.$ Here, since $y_0=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-\\lambda Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-\\lambda Δx)((1-\\lambda Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & =(1-\\lambda Δx)^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & =(1-\\lambda Δx)^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =(1-\\lambda Δx)^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's define $R(\\lambda\\Delta x) = 1 - \\lambda\\Delta x.$ Then, $y_n$ converges to $0$ as $n \\to \\infty$ if and only if $|R(\\lambda\\Delta x)| < 1.$ The function $R(\\lambda\\Delta x)$ is called the **stability function** of Euler's method.\n\nExpanding this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1 \\\\ -1<1-\\lambda Δx & <1 \\\\ -2<-\\lambda Δx & <0 \\\\ 0<\\lambda Δx & <2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0 < \\lambda\\Delta x < 2.$\n\nThe diagram below shows the exact solution curve with the numerical steps of Euler's method for the cases $\\lambda\\Delta x < 2$ and $\\lambda\\Delta x = 2,$ with $\\lambda > 0$ fixed.\n\n![](images/30/30dc27c192c43aa4fb57ed79f8412b85d106e662ce0416f814621d482d2466ea.png)\n\nNotice that, for $\\lambda\\Delta x < 2,$ the numerical values converge to $0$ (stable), whereas for $\\lambda\\Delta x = 2,$ the values oscillate between $-1$ and $1$ and do not converge to $0$ (unstable).\n\nFor values $\\lambda\\Delta x > 2,$ the numerical solution would grow in magnitude at each step, leading to rapidly diverging values." :tutorial/id #uuid "ee73cf90-ef1e-5e56-b1f5-449c9ca0a73f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14463 :tutorial/title "Stability of Euler's Method"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230182 :tutorial/content "In general, when a numerical method\n$y_{n+1} = y_n + \\Delta y_n$\nis applied to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0) = 1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0,\n$$\n\nwe can write the update in the form\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = R(\\lambda\\Delta x)y_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda\\Delta x)$ is the **stability function** of the method.\n\nFor stability, we require $y_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty.$ Here, since $y_0=1,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(R(\\lambda Δx))^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & =(R(\\lambda Δx))^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =(R(\\lambda Δx))^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a geometric sequence, this decays to $0$ as $n\\to\\infty$ if and only if $|R(\\lambda\\Delta x)| < 1.$\n\nThe set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda\\Delta x)| < 1,$ is called the **stability region** of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda\\Delta x > 0$ is called **absolutely stable**.\n\nWe've seen that Euler's method is *not* absolutely stable, since its stability region is $0 < \\lambda\\Delta x < 2.$ But what about the implicit Euler method? Let's find out." :tutorial/id #uuid "b296dcea-4d6f-59ab-8621-78aab4024f7d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14464 :tutorial/title "Stability Functions, Stability Regions, and Absolute Stability"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230183 :tutorial/content "A table of some numerical methods, their stability functions, and stability regions is shown below. These results assume the test equation $y' = -\\lambda y$ (where $\\lambda > 0$) with step size $\\Delta x$.\n\n| Numerical Method | Stability Function $R(\\lambda \\Delta x)$ | Stability Region $|R(\\lambda \\Delta x)| < 1$ | Absolutely Stable? |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Euler's | $1 - \\lambda \\Delta x$ | $0 < \\lambda \\Delta x < 2$ | $\\color{red}\\times$ |\n| Implicit Euler | $\\dfrac{1}{1 + \\lambda \\Delta x}$ | All values $\\lambda \\Delta x > 0$ | $\\color{green}\\checkmark$ |\n| Trapezoidal | $\\dfrac{1 - \\lambda \\Delta x/2}{1 + \\lambda \\Delta x/2}$ | All values $\\lambda \\Delta x > 0$ | $\\color{green}\\checkmark$ |\n| Modified Euler | $1 - \\lambda\\Delta x + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^2}{2}$ | $0 < \\lambda\\Delta x < 2$ | $\\color{red}\\times$ |\n| Runge-Kutta (RK4) | $1 - \\lambda\\Delta x + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^2}{2} - \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^3}{6} + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^4}{24}$ | $0 < \\lambda\\Delta x < 2.785\\ldots$ | $\\color{red}\\times$ |" :tutorial/id #uuid "58221058-7c32-5e60-97f1-2702853713f8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14470 :tutorial/title "Table of Results"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230185 :tutorial/content "Now, let's check the stability of the implicit Euler method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0) = 1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that, for an initial value problem $y' = f(x,y), y(x_0) = y_0,$ the implicit Euler method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = y_n + f(x_{n+1},y_{n+1}) \\cdot \\Delta x.\n$$\n\nApplying the implicit Euler method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-\\lambda {y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+\\lambda {y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1}(1+\\lambda Δx) & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is \n\n$$\nR(\\lambda\\Delta x) = \\dfrac{1}{1 + \\lambda\\Delta x}.\n$$\n\nThe method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y_n$ converges to $0$ as $n \\to \\infty$ if and only if\n$\\left|\\dfrac{1}{1 + \\lambda\\Delta x}\\right| < 1.$\n\nSince $1 + \\lambda\\Delta x > 1$ for any positive step size $\\Delta x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\left|\\dfrac{1}{1 + \\lambda\\Delta x}\\right| < 1\n$$\n\nfor all $\\Delta x > 0.$ So, the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable!\n\nThe diagram below shows the exact solution curve with the numerical steps of both the explicit and the implicit Euler methods for the value $\\lambda\\Delta x = 2.$ \nWe saw before that the explicit method oscillates between the values $-1$ and $1$ and does not converge to $0.$\n\nConversely, the numerical steps of the implicit Euler method converge to $0$ quite quickly for the same step size!\n\n![](images/08/083b5c0193a2a35648434cae4dcf1820fd47c86202c13a7e7e2e078d45d52bdb.png)\n\nImplicit methods tend to be more numerically stable than explicit methods.\n\nThe diagram below shows the exact solution curve with the numerical steps of both the explicit and the implicit Euler methods for the value [math]\\lambda\\Delta x < 2.[/math] \nThe numerical steps of the implicit Euler method converge to [math]0[/math] quicker than for the explicit Euler method for the same step size!\n\n<div id=\"graphic-813b2625835b093104989990215db68f\" class=\"graphic\"><img src=\"/graphics/813b2625835b093104989990215db68f\" /></div>\n\nWhich of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the implicit Euler method on the test equation [math]y'=-\\lambda y[/math] with [math]y = y(x), y(0) = 1, x \\geq 0, \\lambda = 8,[/math] and step size [math]\\Delta x?[/math]  \n\nI. The stability function is [math]R(8\\Delta x) = \\dfrac{1}{1-8\\Delta x}.[/math]  \nII. The stability region is [math]\\left|\\dfrac{1}{1 + 8\\Delta x}\\right| < 1.[/math]  \nIII. The method is absolutely stable.  \nIV. The method is **unstable** for step size [math]\\Delta x = 0.05.[/math]  \n\n*Hint:* The stability function [math]R[/math] is given by [math]y_{n+1} = R(\\lambda\\Delta x)y_n.[/math]\n\nA numerical method \n[math]\ny_{n+1} = y_n + \\Delta y_n\n[/math]\nis stable for a given step size [math]\\Delta x[/math] if, when applied to the test equation\n\\[\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0) = 1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0,\n\\]\nthe numerical solution satisfies [math]y_n \\to 0[/math] as [math]n \\to \\infty,[/math] just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\\[\ny_{n+1} = R(\\lambda\\Delta x)y_n,\n\\]\nwhere [math]R(\\lambda\\Delta x)[/math] is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by [math]|R(\\lambda\\Delta x)| < 1,[/math] is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values [math]\\lambda\\Delta x > 0[/math] is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the implicit Euler method by applying it to the test equation\n\n\\[\ny' = -8y, \\qquad y(0) = 1.\n\\]\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem [math]y' = f(x,y), y(x_0) = y_0,[/math] the implicit Euler method is given by the update formula\n\n\\[\ny_{n+1} = y_n + f(x_{n+1},y_{n+1}) \\cdot \\Delta x.\n\\]\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n* Applying the implicit Euler method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\\begin{align*}\ny_{n+1} &= y_n + (-8 y_{n+1}) \\cdot \\Delta x \\\\[5pt]\ny_{n+1} + 8y_{n+1} \\cdot \\Delta x &= y_n \\\\[5pt]\ny_{n+1}(1 + 8\\Delta x) &= y_n \\\\[5pt]\ny_{n+1} &= \\dfrac{1}{1 + 8\\Delta x}\\cdot y_n\n\\end{align*}\nSo, the stability function is \n\\[\nR(8\\Delta x) = \\dfrac{1}{1 + 8\\Delta x}.\n\\]\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n* The method is stable when\n\\begin{align*}\n|R(8\\Delta x)| &< 1 \\\\[5pt]\n\\left|\\dfrac{1}{1 + 8\\Delta x}\\right| &< 1.\n\\end{align*}\nTherefore, [math]y_n[/math] converges to [math]0[/math] as [math]n \\to \\infty[/math] if and only if\n[math]\n\\left|\\dfrac{1}{1 + 8\\Delta x}\\right| < 1.\n[/math]\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n* Now, since [math]1+8\\Delta x > 1[/math] for any positive step size [math]\\Delta x,[/math] we have\n\\[\n\\left|\\dfrac{1}{1 + 8\\Delta x}\\right| < 1\n\\]\nfor all [math]\\Delta x > 0.[/math] So, the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, and statement III is true.\n\n* Finally, because the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for step size [math]\\Delta x = 0.05.[/math] Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." :tutorial/id #uuid "1648c2df-353a-5461-abab-2c546f249a47" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14484 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"}]}
